","The maximum height is reached when the velocity of the particle becomes zero. Using the formula for velocity \( v = u + u_t \), where \( u \) is the initial velocity, \( u_t \) is the acceleration, and \( t \) is the time, we find the time taken to reach maximum height:
\[
v = 0 = 20 - 9.8t
\]
\[
t = \frac{20}{9.8} \approx 2.04 \, \text{s}
\]
Now, we use the formula for displacement \( s = u t + \frac{1}{2} u_t t^2 \), where \( s \) is the distance, \( u \) is the initial velocity, \( t \) is the time, and \( u_t \) is the acceleration. Substituting the values:
\[
s = 20 \times 2.04 + \frac{1}{2} \times (-9.8) \times (2.04)^2
