</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
To calculate the power output of the Galvanic cell, we first need to determine the cell potential (E_cell) under non-standard conditions using the Nernst equation:

E_cell = E°_cell - (RT/nF) * ln(Q)

where:
E°_cell = standard cell potential
R = gas constant (8.314 J/mol·K)
T = temperature (298 K)
n = number of electrons transferred in the redox reaction (2 for this case, as Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu and 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂)
F = Faraday's constant (96485 C/mol)
Q = reaction quotient

The standard cell potential (E°_cell) can be calculated by subtracting the standard reduction potential of the anode (hydrogen) from that of the cathode (copper):

E°_cell = E°_cathode - E°_anode = 0.34 V - 0.0 V = 0.34 V

