</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
The sulfides that precipitate are \( \text{CuS} \) and \( \text{HgS} \). This is because \( \text{CuS} \) and \( \text{HgS} \) have very low solubility product constants (\( K_{sp} \)), making them insoluble even in acidic conditions. On the other hand, \( \text{MnS} \) and \( \text{NiS} \) have higher \( K_{sp} \) values and remain soluble in the acidified solution. Thus, the correct answer is \(\boxed{\text{CuS and HgS}}\).
</GROUND_TRUTH>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
When \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) gas is passed into a mixture of \( \text{Mn}^{2+}, \texttext{Ni}^{2+}, \text{Cu}^{2+}, \) and \( \text{Hg}^{2+} \) ions in an acidified aqueous solution, the sulfides of these ions will precipitate if their solubility product (Ksp) exceeds the solubility product of \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) in the solution. The solubility product of \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) is very small, so even to form insoluble sulfides, the solubility product of the sulfide must be very low. 

The correct answer is:  
1. **CuS**: \( \text{Cu}^{2+} + \text{S}^{2-} \rightarrow \text{CuS} \)  
2. **HgS**: \( \text{Hg}^{2+} + \text^{2-} \rightarrow \text{HgS} \)  

