- Let \( b \) is the speed of B (60 cm/s)
- Let \( t \) be the time after B startssartsss
- Let \( d \) be the distance at which B collches with A.

When B starts, A is already moving. After 3 hours, A's distance is:
\[
\text{Distance (A)} = 20 \text{ cm/s} \times 3 \text{ hours} = 60 \text{ km}
\]
The distance at which B starts is 60 km.

The speed at which B converges with A is the difference of their speeds:
\[
\text{Speed of collision} = \text{Speed (B)} - \text{Speed (A)} = 20 \text{ km/min} - 10 \text{ km/min} = 10 \text{ km/min}
\]
The time \( t \) for B to collide with A is:
\[
t = \frac{\text{Distance (A)}}{\text{Speed of collision}} = \frac{60 \text{ km}}{10 \text{ km/min}} = 6 \text{ s}
\]
The distance at which B collches with A is:
\[
\text{Distance (B)} = \text{Speed (B)} \times t = 20 \text{ km/min} \times 6 \text{ s} = 120 \text{ km}
\]
Thus, B collches with A at 120 km from the start.

