</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the work done during the displacement of the car, we use the formula:

\[
W = F \cdot d \cdot \cos \theta
\]

Where:
- \( W = \text{work done} \).
- \( F = \text{force applied} \).
- \( d = \text{distance traveled} \).
- \( \theta = \text{the angle between the force and the displacement} \).

Substituting the given values:
- \( F = 60.0 \, \text{N} \)
- \( d = 10.0 \, \text{m} \)
- \( \theta = 60.0° \)

\[
W = 60.0 \, \text{N} \times 10.0 \, \text{m} \times \cos 60.0°
\]

Since \( \cos 60.0^\circ = 0.5 \):

\[
W = 60.0 \times 10.0 \times 0.5 = 300 \, \text{J}
\]

Thus, the work done is \(\boxed{300 \, \text{J}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

