1. Surface modification: Plasma-based nanostructure formation can enhance the light-to-bandband ratio of solar cells, which can lead to improved efficiency. For example, plasma-assisted nanostructuring of the surface of the semiconductor material can enhance the light-to-body ratio of solar cells, which can lead to improved efficiency.

2. Thin-layer fabrication: Plasma-based techniques can be used to fabricate thin-layer materials for solar cells, such as thin-metal, thin-metal-organic, and thin-metal-organic-organic. These thin-layer materials can be used to improve the performance of solar cells by enhancing their performance, reducing their cost, and extending their lifespan.

3. Surface passivation: Plasma-based treatment can create a protective layer on the semiconductor surface, preventing the formation of a thin oxide layer, which can improve the performance of solar cells by enhancing their performance, reducing their cost, and extending their lifetime.

