\[
a_p = r \omega
\]
Given \( a_p = 2 \text{ m s}^{-2} \) and \( r = 0.5 \text{ m} \), the angular acceleration \( \omega \) is:
\[
\omega = \frac{a_p}{r} = \frac{2 \text{ m s}^{-2}}{0.5 \text{ m}} = 4 \text{ rad s}^{-2}
\]
The net torque on the pulley is given by:
\[
T = I \omega,
\]
where \( I \) is the moment of inertia of the disc and \( \omega \) is the angular acceleration. For a disk, the moment of inertia is:
\[
I = \frac{1}{2} m r^2 = \frac{1}{2} \times 1 \text{ kg} \times (0.5 \text{ m})^2 = 0.025 \text{ kg m}^2
\]
The torque due to the force \( F \) is:
\[
T = F r
\]
Equating the two expressions for the torque:
\[
F r = I \omega
\]
Solving for \( F \):
\[
F = \frac{I \omega}{r} = \frac{0.025 \text{ kg m}^2 \times 4 \text{ rad s}^{-2}}{0.5 \text{ m}} = 0.01 \text{ N}
\]

Thus, the magnitude of the force \( F \) is \( \boxed{0.01 \text{ N}} \).
