","The magnetic field strength \( B \) at the center of a circular coil is given by the formula:

\[
B = \frac{\mu \mu_0}{2\pi r} \times N \times r = \frac{\mu \mu_0}{2\pi} \times N \cdot r^2
\]

where:
- \( \mu \) is the permeability of the material of the coil (assuming the coil is made of a non-conducting material, \( \mu = 1 \))
- \( \mu_0 \) is the permeability of free space, which is \( 4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T·m} \, \text{m}^2 \, \text{A}^{-1} \).
- \( r \) is the radius of the coil.
- \( N \) is the number of turns in the coil.

Substituting the given values:
- \( N = 100 \) turns
- \( I = 1 \, \text{A} \)
- \( r = 0.2 \, \text{m} \)

The magnetic field strength is:

\[
B = \frac{\mu \mu_0}{2\pi r} \times N \times r = \frac{1 \times 4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T·m} \, \text{m}^2 \, \text{A}^{-1}}{2\pi \times 0.2 \, \text{m}} \times 100 \times 0.2 \, \text{m}
\]

Simplifying:

\[
B = \frac{4 \times 10^{-7} \times 100 \times 0.2}{2 \times 0.2} = 2 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T}
\]

Thus, the magnetic field strength at the center of the coil is \( \boxed{2 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T}} \).",fluency,"# Task Description
