(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","(a) The acceleration of the proton is given by the formula \( F = F_g = F_g = \frac{F}{m} \), where \( F \) is the force on the proton due to the electric field, \( m \) is the mass of the proton, and \( a \) is the acceleration.  
The force on the proton is \( F = qE \), where \( q \) is the charge of the proton and \( E \) is the magnitude of the electric field.  
Substituting \( F = qE \) into the equation \( F = ma \), we get \( qE = ma \), so \( a = \frac{qE}{m} \).  
Substituting the given values:  
\[
a = \frac{(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C})}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.79 \times 10^{8} \, \text{m/s}^2
\]  
The acceleration of the proton is \(\boxed{4.79 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}^2}\).  

(b) The acceleration \( a \) is constant, and the proton starts from rest, so \( v = a t \), where \( v \) is the velocity, \( a \) is the acceleration, and \( t \) is the time.  
Solving for \( t \):  
\[
